---
/* YOUR FIRST SESSION — 04/10/2026, Session 28. The questions of a skater's first visits
   that are about the rink rather than the kit. Martyn: emphasise never standing on
   metal, which is at the edges of some temporary seasonal rinks and in the bleachers. */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url.js';
---
<Base title="Your first session" subtitle="Getting on and off the ice, which way to skate, where to stop, and never standing on metal.">
  <h1>Your first session</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What nobody tells you before your first few visits to a rink, from the changing area
    to the ice and back.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you step on</h2>
  <p>
    Lace up away from the ice door, so you are not in the way of people getting on and off.
    Walk to the ice in your guards. Take them off at the door, put them somewhere you will
    find them, and step on holding the barrier. Taking the guards off before that first
    step matters: a guard on the ice stops dead and the skater does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    What to bring besides skates is on <a href={url('kit/bag/')}>the kit bag</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="warn" role="note">
    <h2>Never stand on metal</h2>
    <p>
      <b>A blade's edge is lost the moment it stands on metal, concrete or stone.</b> One
      step can blunt it or chip it, and an edge that has gone will not hold until the blade
      is sharpened again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Metal is easy to miss around a rink. Temporary and seasonal rinks often have metal
      frames, edging and thresholds at the side of the ice, and the step from the ice to
      the walkway may be one of them. Seating and bleachers around a rink often have metal
      steps and floors. Door sills, drain covers and the doors themselves are metal too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep to the rubber matting, and put your guards on for anything else. If there is
      no matting between the ice and where you are going, the guards go on at the ice
      door.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Which way to skate</h2>
  <p>
    A public session skates round the rink in one direction, usually anticlockwise. Follow
    the signs and the stewards, and go with everyone else. Keep to the outside while you
    find your feet, and if you need the barrier, use it: that is what it is for.
  </p>

  <h2>Stopping and standing</h2>
  <p>
    Stop at the side, beside the barrier, not in the middle of the ice and never in front of
    the door. Skaters coming round behind you are looking ahead, not for someone standing
    still. If you need a rest, step off the ice.
  </p>

  <h2>Falling</h2>
  <p>
    Everyone falls, coaches included. Keep your hands close in and your fingers off the ice
    while you are down, because other skaters' blades are the real danger, and get up
    straight away. The <a href={url('elements/falling-and-getting-up/')}>falling and getting
    up</a> page shows how.
  </p>

  <h2>Kinds of session</h2>
  <p>
    A public session is for everyone, at every level. A freestyle or practice session is
    for skaters training jumps, spins and programmes, and is usually booked through a
    club or a coach. On those, the skater whose music is playing usually has priority, and
    some rinks mark them with a sash or a bib. Lessons may share a public session in a
    coned-off area, or have the ice to themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most sessions stop for the ice to be resurfaced. Leave when you are asked, guards on at
    the door.
  </p>

  <h2>After the session</h2>
  <p>
    Guards on at the door, dry the blades as soon as you sit down, and again later. The
    rest is on <a href={url('kit/care/')}>looking after skates</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
<style>
  .warn{margin:1.6rem 0;padding:.4rem 1rem .6rem;border:2px solid var(--accent);border-radius:10px;background:var(--ice)}
  .warn h2{margin-top:.6rem}
</style>
